{"parentUuid":"ce4f5bd9-1aac-4b20-be48-345ce3fac03a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfzru9C86SbDJLsHpoVHn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# The Rhythm of the Sea: Understanding Tides\n\nTwice a day, in most places, the ocean breathes. Water creeps up the beach, swallows the rocks, and fills the harbors, then slowly withdraws and leaves mudflats, tide pools, and stranded boats. This rhythm is so familiar to coastal people that it can seem as natural as sunrise. Yet the tides are one of the most elegant demonstrations of physics on Earth. They link the oceans to the Moon and the Sun, and they shape life, commerce, and landscape along every shoreline.\n\nThe primary cause of tides is gravity. The Moon pulls on everything on Earth, but the strength of that pull depends on distance. Water on the side of the planet facing the Moon is pulled more strongly than the solid Earth beneath it, and the Earth is in turn pulled more strongly than the water on the far side. The result is two bulges of ocean, one facing the Moon and one pointing directly away. As the Earth rotates beneath these bulges, a given coastline passes through both of them each day, which produces two high tides and two low tides. Because the Moon also orbits the Earth, a full cycle takes about twenty-four hours and fifty minutes. This is why tides arrive roughly fifty minutes later each day.\n\nThe Sun matters too. Although it is v"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":44,"cache_read_input_tokens":59835,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":44},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cfzru8tkTSMAbx1ipL33b","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assista